      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film brilliantly demonstrates how creative constraints can spark innovation. Each \"obstruction\" forces Leth to radically reimagine his work - from shooting in Cuba with no shot longer than 12 frames, to filming in \"the worst place in the world,\" to making a cartoon version. It's reminiscent of how the Dogme 95 movement (which von Trier co-founded) used strict rules to force creative solutions, seen in films like <a href=\"/movie/134686\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"134686\">The Celebration</a> (1998) and <a href=\"/movie/42881\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"42881\">Julien Donkey-Boy</a> (1999)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Von Trier's sadistic challenges reveal as much about him as they do about Leth. Like Hearts of Darkness (1991) documented Francis Ford Coppola's descent into obsession while making Apocalypse Now, The Five Obstructions captures von Trier's complicated relationship with his mentor and his own artistic demons. The film becomes a fascinating study of creative process, artistic ego, and the teacher-student dynamic. SUBHEAD: The Art of Creative Constraints"
      },
      {
        "type": "movies",
        "movies": [
          {
            "title": "Hearts of Darkness: A Filmmaker's Apocalypse",
            "year": 1991,
            "slug": "Documentary chronicling the troubled production of Apocalypse Now",
            "poster": "https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w500/gJSSzYSLXIM5LQMWTOdsYCgawZ5.jpg",
            "initialStreaming": "Showtime",
            "tmdb_id": 4539,
            "poster_url": "https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w500/gJSSzYSLXIM5LQMWTOdsYCgawZ5.jpg"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's exploration of artistic limitations connects to other experimental works that embrace constraints, like <a href=\"/movie/16646\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"16646\">Russian Ark</a> (2002) with its single 96-minute take, or <a href=\"/movie/36234\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"36234\">Timecode</a> (2000) with its four simultaneous continuous shots. Even mainstream films like <a href=\"/movie/1580\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1580\">Rope</a> (1948) and Birdman (2014) have used technical restrictions to push creative boundaries. Von Trier's \"obstructions\" prove that limitations often lead to the most innovative solutions."
      }
